  <h2>What I wish I'd known</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Not all GLP-1s are the same.</strong> Ozempic and Mounjaro work through different mechanisms. A bad experience on one doesn't mean the whole class is off the table.</li>
    <li><strong>Three months is a fair trial.</strong> If you're still miserable after 8–12 weeks with no improvement, that's worth a serious conversation with your doctor — not something to just push through.</li>
    <li><strong>The washout period is telling.</strong> That week between stopping Ozempic and starting Mounjaro showed me how much I'd been putting up with. If you've forgotten what "normal" feels like, that's a red flag.</li>
    <li><strong>Protein becomes a real issue.</strong> Once the appetite suppression is working, eating enough protein takes conscious effort. I started tracking around month four and it made a noticeable difference to my energy.</li>
    <li><strong>Be honest with your doctor.</strong> I spent weeks downplaying my side effects because I thought I should just push through. When I finally told my GP how bad it really was, she suggested the switch immediately. I wish I'd spoken up sooner.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Thinking about switching?</strong> Switching GLP-1 medications is more common than you might think. Mounjaro isn't the only alternative — Zepbound (also tirzepatide) is the weight-management version, and there are other options in the pipeline. The important thing is to have an honest conversation with your prescriber rather than silently suffering or stopping treatment altogether.
  </div>

  <h2>Where I'm going from here</h2>

  <p>I'm staying on Mounjaro at 10mg for now. My doctor and I will reassess in another six months. My A1C target is below 6.5 — I'm not far off. The weight loss has been significant but I'm more focused on how I feel day to day than the number on the scale.</p>

  <p>The biggest change isn't physical. It's that I don't dread my injection day anymore. On Ozempic, I'd stare at the pen on the kitchen counter and think, "here we go again." On Mounjaro, it's just part of the routine — quick, painless, and I get on with my day.</p>

  <p>If you're reading this because you're struggling on your current medication, please don't give up. Talk to your doctor. There are options. What nearly made me quit turned out to be a medication problem, not a me problem.</p>

